If you've never seen a chiropractor before, it's natural to wonder what will happen. Knowing what to expect can make the visit more comfortable — and help you get the most out of it. Here's how a first appointment works at Malak Chiropractic.

## Before you arrive

You can book online through our booking page. When you book, you'll be asked for some basic details and may be sent an intake form to complete beforehand. This gives us a head start on your health history, so more of the appointment can be spent on you.

**It helps to bring:**

- A list of any medications and supplements you take
- Any relevant scans, reports or referral letters (you don't need imaging to book)
- Your private health fund card if you'd like to claim on the spot
- Your GP referral letter if you're attending under a Medicare Chronic Condition Management Plan, workers compensation or DVA

**What to wear:** comfortable clothing you can move in, such as activewear. You won't need to undress for most assessments, and we'll always explain and ask before examining any area.

## Step 1: Your story

The most important part of the first appointment is the conversation. We'll ask about:

- What's bothering you, when it started and how it has changed
- What makes it better or worse
- How it affects your sleep, work, exercise and daily life
- Your general health, past injuries, surgeries and medications
- What you're hoping to get out of care

This history helps us form an initial picture of what's going on, and — just as importantly — screen for any signs that need medical attention instead of, or as well as, chiropractic care.

## Step 2: Examination

Next comes a physical examination tailored to your concern. This may include:

- Watching how you move, bend and turn
- Gentle hands-on assessment of joints and muscles
- Orthopaedic tests that place specific structures under load
- A neurological screen — checking reflexes, sensation and strength — where symptoms suggest nerve involvement

Everything is explained as we go, and you can ask us to pause or stop at any point.

**Do you need X-rays?** Usually not. Guidelines recommend against routine imaging for most back pain, because scans often show changes that are common in people without pain and rarely change management. Imaging is considered when there are signs of possible serious pathology or when results would change your care, and we'd coordinate this with your GP.

## Step 3: Explanation and options

We'll explain what we think is contributing to your symptoms in plain language, and what that means. Then we'll discuss your options, which may include:

- Hands-on treatment such as spinal manipulation, lower-force techniques, soft tissue therapy or dry needling
- An exercise and self-management plan
- Advice on activity, work set-up and sleep
- Referral to your GP or another health professional
- Doing nothing for now and monitoring — sometimes a perfectly reasonable option

We'll also talk about the expected benefits, the risks relevant to you, roughly how long improvement may take and what it will cost.

## Step 4: Consent and treatment

Treatment is only provided where it's clinically appropriate **and** you consent. Consent is ongoing — you can ask questions, decline any technique, or change your mind at any time, including partway through.

If we're both comfortable to proceed, you may receive treatment during the first visit. Some people feel some mild soreness or stiffness for a day or two afterwards, similar to after exercise. Let us know if anything concerns you.

## After your appointment

You'll leave with a clear idea of what's going on and what happens next. That might be a few follow-up visits with specific goals, some exercises to practise, or a referral elsewhere. We'll review your progress regularly and adjust — or stop — care if it isn't helping.

## Questions we often hear

**Will it hurt?** Assessment should not be painful, though some tests may briefly reproduce your symptoms — that's useful information. We'll always work within your comfort.

**Will I hear a "crack"?** Some manipulation techniques produce a popping sound, which is gas releasing within the joint. It isn't bones moving out of or into place. If you'd prefer not to have manipulation, there are other options.

**Do I need to keep coming forever?** No. Our aim is to help you improve and manage independently, with a plan that has clear goals and review points.

If you have questions before booking, get in touch — we're happy to help.
